ut because  what's here right now there's a diaspora  of blogs of internet sites I mean yes  they're sources but there's no one  unifying voice and this lack of a of a  strong voice in this space here is in  this gap that almost is reflected in a  lack of political representation here  and this gap this gap becomes polarizing  I have reached out to a lot of campus  conservatives to try and better  understand that the political space in  the US and there were two stories that  really stuck out for me one was a  Republican and he described to me his  pain in the last election he said döner  my choices were the mccain-palin ticket  or Obama and despite being a Republican  he couldn't bring himself to vote for  the candidates that his party had put up  he felt that mccain-palin just simply  didn't represent the values that he  believed in he called himself a liberal  Republican and he ended up voting for  Obama  there was another campus conservative he  was actually part of the original Tea  Party movement he told he was very  excited as he described it he said we  were out there in the town hall meetings  reading the Constitution it was almost  like a pure intellectual movement  small-government about getting back to  basics about the individual rights but  what happened  he had Fox News Network pick up the  coverage picking up this movement then  he had Sarah Palin come in the movement  became popular and it shifted out to the  right and this campus conservative told  me I could no longer associate myself  with this movement it felt that this  movement has shifted out to a hardcore  fiscal conservative position into a  socially conservative position and he  felt that it was homeless became anti  intellectual and this is what's  happening here people here in this space  people here are being pushed out you  either have to go right or you have to  go left because there's no  representation here and that's not a  good thing  to add to this polarization this is  combination of sensationalism of poor  journalism and almost a repetition that  comes with the 24-hour news format this  combination has the potential to make  the polarization worse in fact I say it  makes the polarization worse let's think  about sensationalism what do we have  here when I started watching some of the  more popular Fox shows I was surprised  at the examples of liberals that they  were bringing into their shows I almost  heard that they were almost the worst  examples of liberals to represent the  other side I remember this one person on  the Hannity show a Michael crook now  this guy believed that it was good US  soldiers were getting killed because  they were getting paid too much  Hannity grew him into the ground as you  can imagine but what is this sensation  isn't doing it makes the Fox viewer here  a much easier to see the other side as  oh my god you are crazy you are  unpatriotic liberals get away from it  and the same thing happens on this side  I've seen that the comedy shows Jon  Stewart Colbert they pick the worst  examples to come out of Fox to make fun  of them and it becomes so easy so easy  to attach that label jeez you guys a  dumb  you guys are rednecks so this  sensationalism just just pushes this  polarization to a new level then this  the repetition the repetition of the  24-hour news format there's obviously  not 24 hours worth of content then so  what ends up happening is the same  content gets repeated again and again  and as advertisers will tell you what  happens when you repeat something again  and again what happens when you repeat  the same image again and again the same  sound again and again well some of that  stuff gets burned into your subconscious  and I can't help but wonder what  influence that's the current media  format now have on us that we're not  even aware of  and lastly poor journalism and and this  is unfortunately I think everywhere now  recently you had MSNBC try to link MIT  Romney to the Ku Klux Klan  they just lifted that out of a blog  unverified and reported it as news what  is that that just gives this side  ammunition ammunition to clearly point  the finger and say how biased are you  and the reverse is true as well  as Fox chops and changes Obama's quotes  to create something entirely new again  makes it so much so much easier for the  people here the point that finger and  say Fox how biased are you so this  combination of sensationalism of poor  journalism of repetition is this is  almost like a nasty cocktail and if you  drink too much of this stuff I say you  start becoming intolerant so now is the  moment of truth I will one day leave the  US and go back to Australia but before I  do that I do want to leave my mark here  I do want to share my cherished lessons  from Flo and Ray those lessons of  tolerance of hearing the other side by  creating a new voice a new voice that  fills this gap this current need a voice  that brings and improves the  polarization a voice that shows good  journalism still has a place in the  media business now I was actually  invited by Anne Grimes  she's the the director of the graduate  program in in journalism to attend a  media entrepreneurship course she's  running in the spring and to try and  breathe life into this dream but I can't  do it alone and this is my ask I'm  looking for three people three people  who share this passion and want to try  and create something new in this special  space  in this special place they can bring out  a tolerant tolerant voice that's not too  far from either side of this political  spectrum and for those of you here who  aren't students we will be needing  advisors so if you share this passion  please I would love to hear your  insights please drop me that business  card and with that I thank you  you
 

